The Jira Scripting API doesn't pick up the user name and password from the Jira instances set up in Jira Administration. Only the Jira Macro uses that configuration. With the scripting API you have to pass the URL and credentials yourself when you get the REST client, for example in Groovy:

def client = services.get("jira").getJiraRestClient(new JIRAServer("${jiraURL}", "${jiraUser}", "${jiraPwd}"))

Fill jiraURL, jiraUser and jiraPwd with your own values. You can then use the client for requests such as creating an issue.

question:
Now, I'd like to create a class that extends the bootstrap classes table-bordered, table-striped, table-condensed and table-hover. Something like :

```
.myClass {
  &:extend(.table-bordered, .table-striped, .table-condensed, .table-hover);
}
```

I quite often apply those 4 classes to tables, so I'd like to have a more concise way to do this, e.g. I'd like to have to apply only 1 class (myClass) instead of all 4.

But it doesn't work. I guess LESS doesn't know where to find those classes (which are defined in skins/flamingo/less/bootstrap/tables.less).

Any idea how to make it work ?

gold.answer:
You don't need `&:extend()`. In the LESS code of the XWiki.StyleSheetExtension object, call the Bootstrap classes as mixins inside your own class:

```
.myClass {
  .table-bordered();
  .table-striped();
  .table-condensed();
  .table-hover();
}
```

Applying `myClass` to a table then gives it the styles of all four Bootstrap table classes at once.

When you save in the WYSIWYG editor, it adds a non-breaking-space paragraph to the empty group. In view mode this renders as <div class="clearfix ..."><p>&nbsp;</p></div>, and that paragraph creates the extra space. Inside the editor, the Flamingo/Sandstone CSS hides the .visible-*-block classes with display:none !important, but that rule doesn't apply in page view mode.

Workaround: create an XWiki.StyleSheetExtension object (a stylesheet loaded on the pages where it's used) that hides the inserted paragraph:

```
.page-break p,
.clearfix p {
    display: none;
}
```

With this, the layout is correct again even though the editor keeps the &nbsp; paragraph in the source.

The column widths differ between the normal view and the PDF export because the PDF export sets explicit column widths before printing. Absolute widths are converted to relative ones, and columns with no width get explicit relative widths. For example, `|left|(% width="200px" %)right` becomes something like `|(% width="35%" %)left|(% width="65%" %)right`. There are two reasons for this. Absolute widths can't be respected in the generated PDF. And without explicit widths, the same column would have different widths on consecutive print pages when a table is split across pages.

The problem is that these relative widths are computed from the screen width, which is usually much larger than the print page. A percentage that looks fine on screen can be too small on paper.

The fix is to limit the content width to the print page width while the widths are computed, using CSS in a custom PDF export template:

```
/* Style applied before printing. */
#xwikicontent {
  /* A4 width, used by default, but may depend on the PDF template. */
  max-width: 210mm;
}

@media print {
  /* ... print styles from the default PDF template ... */
  #xwikicontent {
    /* No need to limit the width once the relative column widths are computed. */
    max-width: none;
  }
}
```

If that still doesn't look right, add custom CSS for your tables. Put it in the skin or color theme to apply it to both view and PDF export, or in a custom PDF export template to apply it to PDF export only.

question:
I tried to change the fielddisplayer for the field "status", so that it shows me the status as text instead of an Icon.

For this I tried out the example in the "Jira Macro" desription, to add a new fielddisplayer by xwiki components.

```
{{groovy}}
import org.xwiki.rendering.block.*
import org.xwiki.rendering.listener.reference.*

def field = xcontext.method.input.get(0)
def issue = xcontext.method.input.get(1)
def parameters = xcontext.method.input.get(2)
String text = xcontext.method.defaultDisplayer.displayField(field, issue,parameters).get(0).getProtectedString()

def reference = new ResourceReference(text, ResourceType.URL)
def labelBlocks = [new WordBlock("Lien")]
xcontext.method.output.value = [new LinkBlock(labelBlocks, reference, true)]
{{/groovy}}
```

For the field "summary" it works perfectly, but "status" seems to ignore it.

```
{{jira fields="status!lien,summary!lien,status!lien" id="6753" source="jql"}}
fixVersion = "Januar- Version 2025"  AND type = Bug
{{/jira}}
```

Dows anyone has an idea how I can change the status too, so it shows the text instead of a Icon?

gold.answer:
The yes/no icons in the rights management UI get their style from the color theme's colors. They're tied to the base color variables @brand-success (green, allowed) and @brand-danger (red, denied). Change those in the color theme's base colors section. Because of how the older derived colors are computed, you may need to restart the instance before the change shows up. If that doesn't work, override the icons with custom CSS, either in a StyleSheetExtension (skin extension) or directly in the Advanced (LESS code) section of the color theme:

#usersandgroupstable button.rights-edit.yes { color: <yourGreen> }
#usersandgroupstable button.rights-edit.no { color: <yourRed> }
